\toc3 Numbering
\mt1 Table of Chapters and Verses
\ip This table is for a reader who looks up a verse of this Old Testament in the King James Bible, or a verse of the King James Bible here. The two number most chapters and verses alike. Where they don’t, the table gives this edition’s numbers beside the King James Bible’s; a book, a chapter, or a verse that it doesn’t name has the same number in both. It takes the place of Brenton’s table of the chapters of Jeremias, which it includes.
\ip The numbers differ for three reasons. The Greek arranges some passages in another order than the Hebrew, as the prophecies of Jeremias against the nations. Each has passages that the other lacks, which are said here to be wanting. And Brenton often divides a verse where the King James Bible doesn’t, so that the verses after it are one higher or lower until the two agree again, and one verse may stand beside two.
\ip A verse that Brenton numbers with a letter, as {PRO 22:8a}, is an addition of the Greek, and has no place in the King James Bible unless the table gives it one.
\is1 The Names of the Books
\ip The King James Bible names these books otherwise, or sets them apart in its Apocrypha, or lacks them.
